  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nClouds cleared quickly across western and central Minnesota this \nmorning, which allowed temperatures to climb into the upper 60s \nto mid 70s before noon. Temperatures are now sitting in the \nupper 70s to low 80s just before 2pm. Further south and east, \nclouds and fog have been slower to clear out, but there should \nstill be plenty of time for widespread highs in the mid to upper\n70s for this last day of September. A more seasonable airmass \nwill arrive following a frontal passage late tonight/early \ntomorrow. Most notably, Tds will tumble from the 60s today down \ninto the upper 30s/40s. Northwest flow and CAA will remain in \nplace into Friday before the surface high slides east and we see\na slight bump in temperatures and moisture Saturday. A \nshortwave will move along the International Border late in the \nday and may lead to a few isolated showers for mainly northern \nand central Minnesota.\n\nThe second half of the weekend, temperatures return to the low\nto mid 60s with ample sunshine. Clear skies and high pressure \noverhead will make for what will likely be the chilliest night \nof the season so far for most. Monday morning lows are currently\nforecast to be in the low 40s for western and southern \nMinnesota with mid 30s across central Minnesota into western \nWisconsin. Following up on the climate stats from the previous \ndiscussion, we typically drop below 50 degrees for first time in\nthe Twin Cities in late August. We have yet to do that this \nyear, but it looks like Friday morning will change that. With a \ncurrent forecast of 42 degrees Monday morning, this would be the\ncoldest morning in the Twin Cities since May 20th (the \nforecasts for STC and EAU also point to it being the coldest \nmorning since mid May). This cool down will be brief with highs \nin the upper 60s to low 70s returning by the end of the period.",
